  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nThe ridge of high pressure that has been parked over the area\nfor the last 10 days will continue to dominate the weather\npattern through the weekend. \n\nOn Wednesday, the ridge axis will slide east of the region,\nallowing a return of SW flow and warmer air pushed into the\nregion. Near normal temps today in the low to mid 50s will climb\ninto the upper 50s to mid 60s Wed, topping out in the upper 60s\nto low 70s by Thursday.\n\nA low pressure system will track along the northern edge of the\nridge across Ontario Thursday, dragging a cold front through \nthe area Thursday night. This front, combined with the warm \nairmass over the area could allow for some rain showers to \ndevelop across the northern Finger Lakes into Oneida county \nduring the overnight hours. Boundary layer moisture may be too \nlow for precipitation to reach the ground, causing virga \ninstead. If we do get a higher influx of moisture that allows \nfor rain to hit the ground, only a trace to a few hundreths of \nan inch are expected.\n\nTemps behind the front will be cooler for Friday and the \nweekend, but still above normal for the end of October. Highs\nwill be in the upper 50s to mid 60s with lows in the upper 30s\nto low 40s.",
